<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roxburgh Castle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roxburgh Castle was a royal castle in the Scottish Borders, on a ridge between the Tweed and the Teviot near Kelso, beside the medieval royal burgh of Roxburgh. It was one of the castles surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-falaise">Treaty of Falaise</a> in 1174 and was held by the English for long periods during the Wars of Independence. James II was killed besieging it in 1460, and the Scots then destroyed it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roxburgh-castle.jpg" alt="Roxburgh Castle" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steve Kent · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castles_old_and_new_-_geograph.org.uk_-_163364.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
